    "problem_statement": "QR code pattern generation produces incorrect output\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen generating QR codes, the positioning patterns appear to be malformed, resulting in incorrect visual output when printed to terminal or converted to ASCII representation.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport qrcode\nimport io\n\n# Create a simple QR code\nqr = qrcode.QRCode(border=0)\nqr.add_data('test')\nqr.make()\n\n# Print ASCII representation\nf = io.StringIO()\nqr.print_ascii(out=f)\nprinted = f.getvalue()\nprint(\"Actual output:\", repr(printed[:10]))\nprint(\"Expected output should start with:\", repr('█▀▀▀▀▀█'))\n\n# Also test TTY output\nf2 = io.StringIO()\nf2.isatty = lambda: True\nqr.print_tty(out=f2)\ntty_printed = f2.getvalue()\nprint(\"TTY output:\", repr(tty_printed[:50]))\n```\n\nThe generated QR code patterns don't match the expected format. The positioning squares and alignment patterns seem to be incorrectly placed or oriented, causing the ASCII and TTY representations to display wrong characters at the beginning of the output."
